From dbf14ee2c1ea4a1822133d65bb85d1bd12bcb8a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Magnus Reimann <magnus.reimann@mailbox.org>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:02:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH gnumach 06/22] lock: make simple_lock_taken meaningful on SMP

On multiprocessors simple_lock_taken always returned 1, so every
assert (simple_lock_taken (...)), vm_object_lock_taken and
vm_page_locked_queues was a no-op on SMP kernels, which are the ones
where missing locks matter most.

The lock word is 1 whenever someone holds the lock, so return it.
This does not tell whether we hold the lock, but it catches the
lock not being held at all.

* kern/lock.h (simple_lock_taken) [NCPUS > 1]: Return the lock word.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 kern/lock.h | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kern/lock.h b/kern/lock.h
index 61781403..1fcc8cf1 100644
--- a/kern/lock.h
+++ b/kern/lock.h
@@ -116,10 +116,11 @@ class	simple_lock_irq_data_t	name = { SIMPLE_LOCK_INITIALIZER(&name.lock) };
 
 /*
  *	The single-CPU debugging routines are not valid
- *	on a multiprocessor.
+ *	on a multiprocessor.  We can still tell whether the lock is
+ *	held at all, even if not whether it is held by us.
  */
 #define	simple_lock_taken(lock)		(simple_lock_assert(lock),	\
-					 1)	/* always succeeds */
+					 (lock)->lock_data)
 #define check_simple_locks()
 #define check_simple_locks_enable()
 #define check_simple_locks_disable()
